[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609091900-splitting-a-protocol-forks-the-contract"
 :adr/title "Splitting a protocol forks the contract"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-09"
 :adr/related "adr-2609091200-a-definition-is-the-unit-of-a-library"
 :adr/body "Measured 2026-09-09 and RESOLVED the same day. A protocol split into a repo of
its own is a DIFFERENT protocol from the one the source namespace declares. An
implementation reified against one is not accepted by the other, and nothing
says so until it is called.

## The measurement that made it concrete

kotoba.lang.fs-host/host-filesystem -- a real JVM filesystem living in the same
repository as kotoba.lang.fs -- answered through the source namespace and failed
through the assembled one:

  No implementation of method: :exists? of protocol:
  #kotoba.fs.filesystem/Filesystem found for class:
  kotoba.lang.fs_host$host_filesystem$reify

Six such implementations existed across these repositories: two in fs, three in
http, one in process. kotoba.lang.process is where it stopped being latent --
its suite exercises the host, so the split failed loudly, while fs and http
reify inside their test files and stayed green with the defect shipped.

## The decision

The source namespace names the split protocol instead of declaring a second one.
Every one of fs, io, http, crypto, device, netcap, test and process now does:

  (def IFilesystem  filesystem-p/Filesystem)
  (def exists?      filesystem-p/exists?)   ; one per method

The methods have to be INTERNED, not referred. A referred var is a mapping, and
a qualified reference like fs/read to a merely referred var does not compile --
No such var: fs/read. The first attempt used :refer and broke fs-host, which is
why that is known rather than assumed.

This inverts what the earlier splits described as purely additive: the source
namespace is now a consumer of its own split. That is what the split was for --
one definition, one identity, everyone referring to it.

## What decided it, rather than who

A copied protocol var works for reify and NOT for extend-type: extend writes
into the original var and dispatch through the copy does not see it. So the
question was whether anything extends these protocols that way. Counted across
the workspace: 46,357 files scanned, 69 reify, ZERO extend-type,
extend-protocol or extend. The change therefore breaks nothing that exists and
repairs sixty-nine implementations, which makes it a repair and not a trade. A
consumer needing extend-type extends the protocol in its own repo, which is the
one these names now point at.

The three options this ADR previously listed are settled: option 2 was taken,
and it did not need an owner-level migration decision because the measurement
removed the cost that made it one.

## The facade still does not re-export a protocol NAME

Methods are functions and copy fine. The protocol name does not, so an
implementer gets a compile error pointing at the one repo that declares the
contract rather than a reify that silently extends nothing. The facades say so
in their own docstrings, alongside the three other things they do not carry:
value vars, record types and macros.

## A name is not bent to fit a host

Every protocol drops its leading I in its own repo: Filesystem, AsyncFilesystem,
Reader, Writer, Http, AEAD, Device, Net, Generator, Process. IProcess was the
one exception for part of a day, because Clojure hands every namespace 96
java.lang names before it says anything and Process is one of them --
(defprotocol Process ...) is Expecting var, but Process is mapped to class
java.lang.Process.

Keeping the I put an accident of one host, the host this workspace ranks last,
into the identity of a definition. The name is uniform now and the grant is
declined where it arises:

  #?(:clj (do (ns-unmap *ns* 'Process)))

which is the principle the library is built on: a component touches only what it
was granted. java.lang.Process stays reachable by its full name -- measured in
both directions, with the form and without it. The 96 names came from
(ns-imports (create-ns (gensym))) on Clojure 1.12.0, not from memory; java.io's
Reader and Writer are not among them, which is why kotoba.io never needed this.

scripts/split-namespace/generate.cljs refuses if a protocol name was kept as-is
to dodge a host collision, and that refusal was checked by reintroducing the old
behaviour and watching it fire by name.

## What this does not change

Every family still matches its unsplit suite exactly. The split does not change
an answer within a family; this was always about the boundary between the split
and code written against the original, and that boundary now has one protocol on
each side of it rather than two."
